  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/d4aac304110ed4743b58ba786141cecc404791130bf35884f8fbefe94c18fbd6.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?",
  "worked_solution": "From the phase line, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along these lines.\n\n- $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-2,2).$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are negative in this region.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are positive in these regions.\n\nThe only slope field that satisfies the above conditions is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/eb76b700066be49d493fe0738b7c3b18b0b34dcaada93f7c961e5647a0a079c1.png)",
